lish speaker that's the  scandinavian languages  that's french spanish portuguese italian  and so forth those are the closest and  easiest  i  group two which is now 30 weeks they've  added another month and a half  would be essentially german  then the next level of difficulty  according to them  is group three  which it doesn't even show up on this  map here uh group four is languages with  significant difficulty  and uh they are  uh oh and i should have they put  romanian in as a quickie 24 weeks so  group 4 is finnish russian the slavic  languages turkish and stuff like that  and that's 44 weeks so it almost doubles  the amount of time required  and the really more difficult languages  like arabic chinese and stuff like that  goes to 88 weeks like it's four times as  long as it takes to learn say swedish  french spanish so  that's what fsi says what has been my  experience well  i think it depends on two things it when  they say x number of weeks that's based  on intensive study that means probably  five hours a day with a teacher  uh so the things that impact it are  three things actually  how similar the language is to a  language you already know  the second thing is how much time you  spend how intensively you study and the  third thing is  um  what was the third thing again what you  want to achieve so what do you what do  you call if you're saying you know is it  fluency you want to achieve or what so  if i look at different languages so  arabic for example  and the reason i came to this was  because of my you know  present experience call it frustrations  depending on the day although i'm  starting to really enjoy the sound of  arabic and i enjoy the letters i'm  enjoying it i'm frustrated that i'm not  better  and i should say that i was very  impressed i managed to find a video of  ollie richards  producing uh egyptian arabic after three  months and i thought wow i'll never be  able to do that it's much like when i  saw judith meyer  speaking hebrew i think after six months  and i thought to myself wow i don't  think i can achieve that so part of it  of course is how  you know  what your goals uh your goals uh what  you want to achieve how much time you  put in and some people might be better  than others so maybe i'm not as good as  ollie richards or judith meyer  but let's take some languages chinese  essentially i was reading the newspaper  reading books  interpreting and stuff after nine to 12  months  but  i had i was at it six seven hours a day  which included three hours a day  one-on-one with a teacher that and i was  being paid as a canadian diplomat so  that was my job so  that was chinese  uh russian which i did on my own because  i'm not being paid to study right now i  study out of interest so i have an hour  an hour and a half i have other  activities sports family  business  friends so it's not a sort of obsessive  you know struggle like what chinese was  so the russian i was a year two years  still having trouble reading  understanding listening  uh speaking  so that was russian now after russian  several years of russian  i decided to do czech well after a year  of check even though the grammar is very  similar maybe  50 of the vocabulary is recognizable  uh  still when i went to prague to try to  activate it  i struggled now i didn't do a lot of  speaking i didn't do a lot of online  speaking i didn't have a one-on-one  teacher nothing i was just learning on  my own much as was the case with russian  although i had more conversations with  evgeny and helena and other russian  tutors at link  so that's even after a year i wasn't  where i would say that i was fluent in  czech despite having had russian but  after check then ukrainian and polish  were easier but still i was at ukrainian  for quite a long while and it took a  visit to levive  and uh  three weeks there with four or five  hours of uh ukrainian speaking every day  and i i would say that i'm a b2 in  ukrainian today  so there again that's a couple of hours  now if i had a tutor  uh three hours a day and another three  hours a day to spend of course  that time period would be shrunk  significantly  so relating the all of that back to  arabic  given that i have an hour and hour and a  half a day  i'm not yet at the stage where i can  just listen away from the computer  because i don't understand the thing i'm  listening to  i think i'll start to gain more traction  as i start to understand more then i'll  be able to activate my my listening in  the car and while exercising and doing  the dishes and stuff i think that my  familiarity with the letters can only  get better it's not going to get worse  so the frustration that forgetting and  not being able to read and stuff will  gradually gradually decline if i'm able  to read more fluently and understand  better when i listen i'll pick up speed  nevertheless at an hour an hour and a  half a day i think uh a couple of years  in arabic i have to be realistic  therefore  i have to enjoy it  enjoy the stuff i'm listening to  uh enjoy and that's why for example  we've now got the first ten stories and  we're having all of the many stories  redone because we had one to ten up at  link but um we only had one to ten and  we were waiting and waiting we didn't  get any more  and the sound quality wasn't ideal and  we now have had someone who's an  egyptian a lady but she reads in modern  standard arabic her voice is very  pleasant the sound is very clear  so  i i know i'm going to be able to enjoy  listening to these stories over and over  again so that's going to help  i  when i was in bratislava at the last  polyglot conference i bought an assy  meal book there because there was a very  persuasive and very friendly gentleman  philippe from asimil who persuaded me  that this was the best possible way to  you know learn arabic so i'm going to  definitely go through those books i  ordered another book because i like  books and i it's fun for me to cover  some of 